  "worked_solution": "To test for continuity of $f(x)$ at a point $x=c$, we need to check three conditions:\n\n- Does $f(c)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)$ exist?\n\n- Does ${lim}_{x\\to c}f(x)=f(c)?$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Using the expression for the middle branch of the function, we obtain that \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, $f(x)$ is defined at $x=0.$ $\\,\\,\u2713$\n\n- Statement II is true. By calculating the one-sided limits, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(x+1) \\\\  & =(0)+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}({e}^{-x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{0} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)\n$$\n\n \nTherefore, the limit of $f(x)$ at $x=0$ exists:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\,\\,\u2713\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Indeed, we have that ${lim}_{x\\to 0}f(x)=1\\ne f(0).$  $\\,\\,\\times $\n\n- Statement IV is false. Since not all statements I-III are true, the function $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
